  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Continuity in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n\nA function $f : U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ is continuous at $x_0$ if for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there's $\\delta > 0$ such that\n\n$$\\|x - x_0\\| < \\delta \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\|f(x) - f(x_0)\\| < \\varepsilon.$$\n\nSame shape as the 1D definition, but now $\\|\\cdot\\|$ is a vector norm (any of the equivalent ones from the previous notebook).\n\nEquivalently: $f$ is continuous at $x_0$ iff for every sequence $x_k \\to x_0$, $f(x_k) \\to f(x_0)$. The **sequential characterisation** is often easiest to use.\n\nThe big new phenomenon vs. 1D: limits along *different paths* into $x_0$ can disagree, so a function with all \"directional limits\" may still be discontinuous. This is the canonical pitfall of multivariable continuity.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 9,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## A function with no limit at the origin\n\nThe textbook example: $f(x, y) = \\dfrac{x y}{x^2 + y^2}$ for $(x, y) \\ne (0, 0)$.\n\nApproach the origin along the line $y = m x$:\n$$f(x, mx) = \\frac{m x^2}{(1 + m^2) x^2} = \\frac{m}{1 + m^2}$$\n\n— independent of $x$! So the limit along each line *exists*, but it depends on $m$. There's no single value $f$ approaches as $(x, y) \\to (0, 0)$, so the function has no limit at the origin (and is therefore not continuous there, regardless of how we extend).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "f_pathological(x, y) := if x = 0 and y = 0 then 0\n                         else (x * y) / (x*x + y*y)$\n\n/* Directional limits */\nm_vals : [0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, -1.0]$\nprint(\"Limit of f(x, m·x) as x → 0, for various m:\")$\nfor m in m_vals do\n  print(\"  m =\", m, \":  m/(1+m²) =\", float(m / (1 + m^2)))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 10,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Limit of f(x, m·x) as x → 0, for various m:\n  m = 0.0 :  m/(1+m²) = 0.0\n  m = 0.5 :  m/(1+m²) = 0.4\n  m = 1.0 :  m/(1+m²) = 0.5\n  m = 2.0 :  m/(1+m²) = 0.4\n  m = -1.0 :  m/(1+m²) = -0.5"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Visualise the function as a 3D surface — the saddle-shape near the origin reveals the path-dependence.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "ax_draw3d(\n  explicit((x*y) / (x^2 + y^2 + 0.0001), x, -1, 1, y, -1, 1),\n  title=\"f(x, y) = xy/(x² + y²) — limit at origin depends on direction\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"y\", zlabel=\"f\",\n  colorscale=\"RdBu\"\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 11,
      "outputs": [
