.4845795853666318,0.4905368951359687,0.494890348795052,0.4978090868964479,0.49945278301156404,0.49997035978188065,0.4994993080340437,0.4981654745751366],[-0.4995401432563133,-0.49997282854772884,-0.49949930803404363,-0.49799912035761507,-0.4953421769520087,-0.4913892808133183,-0.48599310035120336,-0.4789997065144047,-0.4702507861692665,-0.4595866414029651,-0.44685006927815285,-0.43189118547527827,-0.4145732042955711,-0.39477911378187763,-0.3724190876139864,-0.3474383579962044,-0.3198251441084466,-0.2896181029964957,-0.25691266411662983,-0.22186554939124087,-0.18469679227081923,-0.14568867145703424,-0.10518117546990596,-0.06356390365497412,-0.021264658079454834,0.021264658079454834,0.06356390365497389,0.10518117546990574,0.14568867145703412,0.1846967922708191,0.22186554939124067,0.2569126641166296,0.28961810299649565,0.31982514410844654,0.3474383579962043,0.3724190876139863,0.3947791137818775,0.4145732042955711,0.43189118547527827,0.44685006927815285,0.459586641402965,0.4702507861692664,0.4789997065144047,0.48599310035120336,0.4913892808133181,0.49534217695200866,0.4979991203576151,0.4994993080340437,0.49997282854772884,0.4995401432563133],[-0.49997500124993743,-0.4995401432563133,-0.49816547457513655,-0.4957366996749195,-0.4921317587370718,-0.4872215752421684,-0.4808712315165286,-0.4729416565659842,-0.46329190934457504,-0.4517821317995703,-0.4382772269086426,-0.422651284800805,-0.4047927327378948,-0.3846101211979183,-0.36203837951621337,-0.33704528436901643,-0.3096377903221353,-0.2798677851274909,-0.2478367682458928,-0.2136989258080546,-0.17766210448376865,-0.1399862813444283,-0.1009792889005504,-0.06098977442122891,-0.02039762803060363,0.02039762803060363,0.06098977442122869,0.10097928890055019,0.1399862813444282,0.17766210448376854,0.21369892580805439,0.2478367682458926,0.27986778512749083,0.30963779032213523,0.3370452843690163,0.36203837951621326,0.3846101211979182,0.4047927327378948,0.422651284800805,0.4382772269086426,0.45178213179957016,0.4632919093445749,0.4729416565659842,0.4808712315165286,0.48722157524216836,0.49213175873707177,0.49573669967491957,0.4981654745751366,0.4995401432563133,0.49997500124993755]],\"type\":\"surface\",\"name\":\"(x*y)/(y^2+x^2+1.0e-4)\"}],\"layout\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"f(x, y) = xy/(x² + y²) — limit at origin depends on direction\"},\"width\":700,\"height\":450,\"paper_bgcolor\":\"white\",\"scene\":{\"xaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"x\"},\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1},\"yaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"y\"},\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1},\"zaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"f\"},\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1}}}}"
            ]
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "display_data"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The values along $y = x$ stay at $1/2$, along $y = -x$ at $-1/2$, along axes at $0$. No way to define $f(0, 0)$ to make it continuous.\n\n## A trickier counter-example\n\nEven more pathological: $g(x, y) = \\dfrac{x^2 y}{x^4 + y^2}$ at $(0, 0)$.\n\nAlong *every line* $y = mx$ (for any $m$), $g(x, mx) \\to 0$ as $x \\to 0$.\n\nBut along the *parabola* $y = x^2$:\n$$g(x, x^2) = \\frac{x^2 \\cdot x^2}{x^4 + x^4} = \\frac{1}{2}.$$\n\nSo directional limits all give 0, but a curved path gives $1/2$ — no limit at origin. Linear paths don't capture the subtleties of multivariable limits.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "g_path(x, y) := if x = 0 and y = 0 then 0 else (x^2 * y) / (x^4 + y^2)$\n\nprint(\"Along y = mx (any m):\")$\nfor m in [0.0, 1.0, 5.0] do block([xs, gs],\n  xs : [0.5, 0.1, 0.01, 0.001],\n  gs : map(lambda([xv], float(g_path(xv, m*xv))), xs),\n  print(\"  m =\", m, \":  g(x, mx) for x =\", xs, \":\", gs))$\n\nprint()$\nprint(\"Along y = x²:\")$\nxs : [0.5, 0.1, 0.01, 0.001]$\ngs : map(lambda([xv], float(g_path(xv, xv*xv))), xs)$\nprint(\"  g(x, x²) for x =\", xs, \":\", gs)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 12,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Along y = mx (any m):\n  m = 0.0 :  g(x, mx) for x = [0.5,0.1,0.01,0.001] : [0.0,0.0,0.0,0.0]\n  m = 1.0 :  g(x, mx) for x = [0.5,0.1,0.01,0.001] :\n     [0.4,0.09900990099009901,0.009999000099990003,9.999990000010002e-4]\n  m = 5.0 :  g(x, mx) for x = [0.5,0.1,0.01,0.001] :\n     [0.09900990099009901,0.019992003198720517,0.0019999920000319995,\n      1.999999920000003e-4]\nAlong y = x²:\n  g(x, x²) for x = [0.5,0.1,0.01,0.001] :\n                  [0.5,0.5000000000000001,0.5,0.4999999999999999]"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Functions that *are* continuous\n\nMost familiar combinations of continuous functions are continuous:\n\n- Sums, products, and quotients (where defined) of continuous functions are continuous.\n- Compositions: if $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ and $g : \\mathbb{R}^m \\to \\mathbb{R}^k$ are continuous, so is $g \\circ f$.\n- Component-wise: $f = (f_1, \\ldots, f_m)$ is continuous iff every $f_j$ is.\n\nPolynomials, $\\sin$, $\\exp$ applied component-wise — all fine. The pathologies show up when you divide by something approaching zero.\n\n## Continuity on a compact set is uniform — Heine-Cantor\n\nIn finite dimensions, a continuous function on a compact set is **uniformly continuous**. Same conclusion as in 1D, same reason: the set is small enough (compact) that a single $\\delta$ works everywhere.\n\nConsequences (parallel to 1D):\n- Continuous on compact ⇒ bounded, attains max and min (Weierstrass).\n- Continuous on compact ⇒ uniformly continuous.\n- Continuous on compact and connected ⇒ image is compact and connected.\n\n## Why \"continuous at every point\" is often not enough\n\nPathological examples like $f(x,y) = xy/(x^2+y^2)$ (defined to be $0$ at origin) is **not** continuous at the origin, but its restriction to every *line through* the origin is continuous (constant, in fact). Continuity is genuinely a multivariable property — *every direction* has to behave, not just lines.\n\nLipschitz, Hölder, and uniform continuity all generalise to $\\mathbb{R}^n$ with the obvious norm modifications. The multidimensional Mean Value Theorem (next notebook) requires more care than the 1D version — there's no single $c$ in higher dimensions, but there's an integral form.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}